"""
unit1_scripts_l14_l15.py
High-volume 20-25 minute broadcast tiki-taka scripts for Lectures 14 and 15.
"""

SCRIPTS_L14 = {
    "1": "Prof. Park: Welcome everyone to Lecture 14 of M090 Introductory Algebra! I am Professor Eunju Park, and joining me at the teaching desk is TA Sora. Today, we turn to Section 1.8 on workbook pages 23 and 24: Solving Formulas for a Specified Variable—Literal Equations.\nTA Sora: Hello everyone! Up until today, most equations had one letter (like x) and several numbers (like 3x + 5 = 20). Today, we encounter formulas that contain MULTIPLE variables—like d = rt, P = 2L + 2W, or Ax + By = C. These are called literal equations or formulas because they are written with letters ('litera' in Latin). Students are often intimidated by literal equations because there aren't many numbers to calculate. They ask: 'How can I solve for y if I don't know what x or z equals?'\nProf. Park: The secret is simple: Treat all other letters exactly like known constants! Ask yourself: 'If that letter were the number 5, what operation would I use to move it?' If you would subtract 5, then subtract x! If you would divide by 3, then divide by m! The algebraic rules never change—only the symbols do!\nTA Sora: In real Montana careers, you do this all the time: In agriculture, irrigation water rights formulas are solved for flow duration t. In forestry and wildlife biology, timber board-foot formulas are solved for usable log diameter d. In construction across Gallatin County, perimeter and load formulas are rearranged to calculate beam length L. Today, we will master isolating any target variable with 100% confidence. Let's begin on slide 2!\nProf. Park: In civil engineering, environmental science, and business management, real equations are almost always literal formulas. For example, the Manning formula calculates open-channel water velocity in irrigation canals across the Gallatin Valley based on channel slope, roughness, and hydraulic radius. An engineer rarely needs to solve for velocity; they already know the target water flow rate, and they need to solve for the channel slope S or roughness coefficient n! Rearranging formulas is the daily bread and butter of working professionals.\nTA Sora: That is why literal equations are so empowering. Once you isolate a formula for your target variable, you can plug in twenty different datasets into a spreadsheet and compute answers instantly without re-doing algebra every single time!",
    "2": "Prof. Park: On slide 2, let's examine Section 1.8 Examples 1A and 1B on workbook page 23: Solving standard linear equations for y. Part A: 3x + y = 8. Part B: 2x - 5y = 12.\nTA Sora: In Part A, we want to isolate y: 3x + y = 8. Look at what is on the same side as y: we have a positive 3x added to y. To eliminate +3x, what is the inverse operation? Subtract 3x from both sides! 3x + y - 3x = 8 - 3x. On the left, 3x - 3x = 0, leaving y isolated! On the right: Can you combine 8 and -3x into 5x? ABSOLUTELY NOT! 8 is a constant and -3x has a variable—they are NOT like terms! You simply write them side-by-side: y = 8 - 3x (or y = -3x + 8). Done in one step!\nProf. Park: Now let's solve Part B: 2x - 5y = 12. We want to isolate y. Step 1: Get the y term (-5y) alone by subtracting 2x from both sides: -5y = 12 - 2x (or -5y = -2x + 12). Step 2: y is multiplied by -5. Divide BOTH sides by -5: y = (-2x + 12) / (-5). Now distribute the division by -5 to both terms: -2x / -5 = +(2/5)x. +12 / -5 = -12/5. So y = (2/5)x - 12/5 (or (2x - 12) / 5). Notice that dividing by -5 changed the signs upstairs: -2x became positive 2x, and +12 became -12. This form—y = mx + b—is the famous slope-intercept form we will use extensively in Unit 2!\nTA Sora: In Example 1B, 2x - 5y = 12, we isolated y to get y = (2/5)x - 12/5. Notice why we split the fraction into two separate terms: In Unit 2, we will graph linear equations using the slope-intercept form: y = mx + b. Here, m = 2/5 is the slope (the steepness of the line: rise 2, run 5), and b = -12/5 is the y-intercept (where the line crosses the vertical axis)! Solving literal equations for y is the exact skill that unlocks all of linear graphing in Unit 2!\nProf. Park: Every topic in Unit 1 is purposefully laying the groundwork for Unit 2. Practice this isolation until it is second nature!",
    "3": "Prof. Park: Slide 3 covers Section 1.8 Examples 1C and 1D on workbook page 23: Part C: Z = x + wxy; solve for y. Part D: 5xy = 19; solve for y.\nTA Sora: Look at Part C: Z = x + wxy. We want to isolate y. Notice where y lives: y is inside the term wxy! Step 1: Isolate the term containing y. Subtract x from both sides: Z - x = wxy. Step 2: What is multiplying y? Both w and x are multiplying y: (wx) · y! The inverse of multiplying by wx is dividing by wx! Divide both sides by wx: (Z - x) / (wx) = y. So y = (Z - x) / (wx)! Can we cancel the x in the numerator with the x in the denominator? NO! Z - x is a subtraction! You can only cancel common factors, never terms attached by minus!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part D: 5xy = 19. Solve for y. y is multiplied by 5 and x: (5x) · y = 19. To isolate y in one step, divide both sides by 5x: y = 19 / (5x). Simple, direct, and completely isolated!\nProf. Park: Let's look closely at Part C: Z = x + wxy; solve for y. Notice why students fail this problem: they try to divide by wx before subtracting x! If you divide by wx first, you would have to divide the x term as well: Z/(wx) = x/(wx) + y. That creates a fraction mess! Always isolate the entire term containing your target variable FIRST by adding or subtracting non-target terms, and ONLY THEN divide by the coefficient factors!\nTA Sora: Isolate the term first, then isolate the variable! That two-step rhythm guarantees success every time.",
    "4": "Prof. Park: On slide 4, let's examine Section 1.8 Examples 2A and 2B on workbook page 23: Solving for specified variable b: Part A: a + b - c = d; solve for b. Part B: ax^2 + bx + c = 0; solve for b.\nTA Sora: In Part A: a + b - c = d. We want to isolate b. What is attached to b? We have +a and -c. To undo +a, subtract a from both sides. To undo -c, add c to both sides! So b = d - a + c! Notice that every term moved across the equal sign simply took its opposite sign: +a became -a, and -c became +c!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part B: ax^2 + bx + c = 0. Solve for b. This looks like the general quadratic equation from advanced algebra! Our target variable is b, which is inside the middle term bx. Step 1: Isolate the term bx by moving all other terms to the right side: Subtract ax^2 from both sides: -ax^2. Subtract c from both sides: -c. So we get: bx = -ax^2 - c. Step 2: To isolate b, divide both sides by x: b = (-ax^2 - c) / x (or b = -ax - c/x). That is our complete solution for b!\nTA Sora: In Part B: ax^2 + bx + c = 0; solve for b. Notice that x appears with an exponent of 2 in ax^2, but our target is b! Don't worry about the x^2—to variable b, ax^2 is just a single package term! Subtract ax^2, subtract c, and divide by x. Treating non-target variable clusters as single numbers is the mark of a mature algebra student.",
    "5": "Prof. Park: Slide 5 presents Section 1.8 Examples 2C and 2D on workbook page 24: Solving fractional formulas for r: Part C: m = (r - 7n) / 5; solve for r. Part D: t = d / r; solve for r.\nTA Sora: Look at Part C: m = (r - 7n) / 5. Our target is r, but r is trapped inside a fraction! How do we free r from the fraction? Clear the fraction! Multiply both sides by the denominator 5: 5 · m = 5 · [ (r - 7n) / 5 ]. On the right, the 5 cancels, leaving: 5m = r - 7n. Now add 7n to both sides to isolate r: r = 5m + 7n! Multiplying by the denominator first immediately freed our variable!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part D: t = d / r. Solve for r. This is the famous distance, rate, and time formula from physics: time = distance / rate! For example, driving 90 miles on Interstate 90 from Bozeman to Butte at speed r takes time t. Notice where r is: r is trapped DOWNSTAIRS in the denominator! You can NEVER solve for a variable while it is in the denominator!\nTA Sora: Step 1: Get r out of the basement! Multiply both sides by r: r · t = r · (d / r) = d. So we get: rt = d (distance = rate times time). Step 2: Now that r is upstairs, isolate r by dividing both sides by t: r = d / t! Speed equals distance divided by time. Clean, intuitive, and physically meaningful!\nProf. Park: Let's look at the physics formula in Part D: t = d / r. If you drive from Bozeman to Billings on Interstate 90, the distance d is about 142 miles. If you want to make the trip in t = 2 hours, what average speed r must you maintain? Using our solved formula r = d / t: r = 142 / 2 = 71 miles per hour! Formulas give you direct control over real physical decisions.\nTA Sora: And notice the common pitfall: students see t = d/r and write r = d · t! Remember: since r was in the denominator, multiplying by r brings it to the numerator on the other side: rt = d. Then dividing by t gives r = d/t!",
    "6": "Prof. Park: On slide 6, we explore geometric formulas from workbook page 24: Part E: P = 2L + 2W; solve for W. Part F: V = LWH; solve for W.\nTA Sora: Part E is the perimeter formula for a rectangle: Perimeter = 2 times Length + 2 times Width. Imagine fencing a horse pasture in the Gallatin Valley: you know the total perimeter fencing P you bought, and you know the pasture length L. How wide can the pasture be? We must solve for W! Step 1: Isolate the 2W term by subtracting 2L from both sides: P - 2L = 2W. Step 2: Divide both sides by 2: W = (P - 2L) / 2 (or W = P/2 - L). Can the 2 in 2L cancel with the 2 in the denominator? NO! P is not multiplied by 2! You cannot cancel unless BOTH terms share the factor 2!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part F: V = LWH. Solve for W. This is the volume formula for a rectangular prism (like a timber beam or concrete foundation footer): Volume = Length times Width times Height. W is multiplied by both L and H: (LH) · W = V. To isolate W, divide both sides by LH: W = V / (LH)! Width equals volume divided by the product of length and height.\nTA Sora: In Part E: P = 2L + 2W; solve for W. Let's see the pasture fencing example with real numbers: Suppose a Gallatin rancher has P = 1,000 feet of wooden post fencing and wants the pasture length to be L = 300 feet. Using our formula W = (P - 2L) / 2: W = (1000 - 2(300)) / 2 = (1000 - 600) / 2 = 400 / 2 = 200 feet! The pasture width must be 200 feet. Formula rearranged once, solved in ten seconds!\nProf. Park: That is the beauty of applied mathematics.",
    "7": "Prof. Park: Slide 7 concludes Lecture 14 with our Section 1.8 Review Problems from workbook page 24: Solving equations by clearing fractions: Review A: (y - 2) / 5 = (3/2)y + 4/5. Review B: 2/7 = (3/7)(x - 4) - 5/7.\nTA Sora: Let's solve Review A: (y - 2) / 5 = (3/2)y + 4/5. Look at the denominators: 5, 2, and 5. The LCD is 10! Multiply every term by 10: 10 · [ (y - 2) / 5 ] = 10 · [ (3/2)y ] + 10 · [ 4/5 ]. First term: 10 / 5 = 2, so 2(y - 2) = 2y - 4. Second term: 10 · (3/2) = 15y. Third term: 10 · (4/5) = 8. So we get: 2y - 4 = 15y + 8. Subtract 2y from both sides: -4 = 13y + 8. Subtract 8 from both sides: -12 = 13y. Divide by 13: y = -12/13!\nProf. Park: Now let's solve Review B: 2/7 = (3/7)(x - 4) - 5/7. All denominators are 7, so the LCD is 7! Multiply every term by 7: 2 = 3(x - 4) - 5. Distribute: 2 = 3x - 12 - 5. Combine like terms: 2 = 3x - 17. Add 17 to both sides: 19 = 3x. Divide by 3: x = 19/3! In Lecture 15, we conclude Unit 1 with Section 1.9: Solving Linear Inequalities and Interval Notation! Fantastic work today, and see you in Lecture 15!\nProf. Park: In Review Problem A: (y - 2)/5 = (3/2)y + 4/5, we multiplied by LCD = 10 to get 2(y - 2) = 15y + 8, leading to y = -12/13. Notice how literal equations and clearing fractions reinforce each other: Both skills require identifying the structural anatomy of an equation and systematically peeling away outer operations. In Lecture 15, we will conclude Unit 1 with inequalities!"
}

SCRIPTS_L15 = {
    "1": "Prof. Park: Welcome everyone to Lecture 15 of M090 Introductory Algebra! I am Professor Eunju Park, and joining me at the teaching desk is TA Sora. Today is a celebration: we complete Section 1.9 on workbook pages 25 through 27: Solving Linear Inequalities, Graphing on Number Lines, and Interval Notation—and we conduct our Grand Unit 1 Review!\nTA Sora: Hello everyone! Today we transition from equations (where two sides are equal) to INEQUALITIES (where one side is strictly greater than, less than, or equal to the other side). In real life, conditions are rarely exact equalities. When civil engineers design the bridge over the Gallatin River on Interstate 90, the load limit is an inequality: Weight ≤ 80,000 pounds. When backcountry skiers check avalanche danger at Bridger Bowl, snowpack temperature must stay below freezing: T < 32° Fahrenheit. Today, we learn how to solve inequalities, express their infinite solution sets on number lines, and write them in standard INTERVAL NOTATION!\nProf. Park: And we must introduce the single most famous rule in all of inequality algebra: The Golden Rule of Inequalities: When you MULTIPLY or DIVIDE both sides of an inequality by a NEGATIVE number, you MUST REVERSE (FLIP) the inequality symbol! If it was less than, it becomes greater than! If it was greater than or equal to, it becomes less than or equal to! Sora, explain why this flip happens with simple numbers.\nTA Sora: Look at 2 and 5. We all agree that 2 < 5. Now multiply both numbers by -1: 2 becomes -2, and 5 becomes -5. Which is larger: owing 2 dollars or owing 5 dollars? -2 is larger than -5! So -2 > -5! The symbol FLIPPED from less-than to greater-than! Multiplying by a negative reflects the numbers across zero to the opposite side of the number line, reversing their relative order! Remember: only flip the symbol when you multiply or divide by a NEGATIVE number. Let's begin on slide 2!\nProf. Park: Let's reflect on the number line to understand why the inequality symbol flips: Imagine a giant mirror sitting right at the origin, zero: x = 0. If you are standing at +3 and your friend is standing at +7, your friend is farther to the right: 3 < 7. Now reflect both of your positions across the mirror by multiplying by -1: You are now at -3, and your friend is at -7! On the negative side of the mirror, who is farther to the right? YOU ARE! -3 is closer to zero and farther right than -7! So -3 > -7! Multiplying by a negative literally flips the entire universe of numbers across the mirror, turning right into left and left into right!\nTA Sora: That mirror analogy makes it impossible to forget! Whenever a negative number multiplies or divides across an inequality, the world flips!",
    "2": "Prof. Park: Slide 2 covers Section 1.9 Examples 1A and 1B from workbook page 25: Basic Interval Notation and Number Line Graphing: Part A: x > 5. Part B: x < -6.\nTA Sora: Let's master our notation conventions: Strict inequalities (< and >) use an OPEN circle on the graph and PARENTHESES ( ) in interval notation because the boundary endpoint is NOT included! Inclusive inequalities (≤ and ≥) use a CLOSED (solid) circle or square bracket [ ] because the boundary endpoint IS included! And infinity (∞) and negative infinity (-∞) ALWAYS take parentheses ( ) because infinity is a direction, not a reachable number!\nProf. Park: Now let's graph Part A: x > 5. On the number line, place an open parenthesis at 5 and shade everything to the RIGHT toward positive infinity. In interval notation, read from left to right: (5, ∞). Now look at Part B: x < -6. Place an open parenthesis at -6 and shade everything to the LEFT toward negative infinity. Reading from left to right: (-∞, -6). Always write the smaller (leftmost) number first in interval notation!\nTA Sora: Let's review interval notation formatting: Interval notation ALWAYS reads from left to right—from smaller numbers to larger numbers! Never write (∞, 5) or (-6, -∞)! Negative infinity is the furthest possible destination to the left, so it must ALWAYS be on the left: (-∞, -6). Positive infinity is the furthest destination to the right, so it must ALWAYS be on the right: (5, ∞)!",
    "3": "Prof. Park: On slide 3, we look at Section 1.9 Examples 1C and 1D on workbook page 25: Inequalities with the variable on the RIGHT side: Part C: -3 ≤ x. Part D: 12 ≥ x.\nTA Sora: Danger alert! When the variable is on the right side, students often shade in the wrong direction! In Part C: -3 ≤ x. Read it from the variable's perspective: 'x is GREATER THAN OR EQUAL TO -3!' If you flip the sides to put x on the left, remember that the inequality mouth stays open toward x: x ≥ -3! Now it is crystal clear: at -3, place a solid bracket [ and shade to the RIGHT! In interval notation: [-3, ∞)!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part D: 12 ≥ x. Read it starting with x: 'x is LESS THAN OR EQUAL TO 12!' Rewriting with x on the left: x ≤ 12. On the number line, place a solid bracket ] at 12 and shade to the LEFT toward -∞. In interval notation: (-∞, 12]! Always rewrite inequalities with the variable on the left if you are ever unsure of the shading direction.\nProf. Park: In Examples 1C and 1D, notice why reading from the variable's perspective is essential: When students read -3 ≤ x from left to right as 'negative 3 is less than or equal to x', they hear the words 'less than' and immediately want to shade to the left! That is a fatal trap! Always turn around and read starting with the variable: 'x is GREATER than or equal to -3!' The wide opening of the inequality symbol faces x, which means x is the larger quantity, pointing to the right!",
    "4": "Prof. Park: Slide 4 covers Section 1.9 Examples 1E and 1F on workbook page 25: Double (Bounded) Inequalities: Part E: -3 < x < 1. Part F: 2 ≤ x < 3.\nTA Sora: A double inequality describes a bounded interval sandwiched between two finite endpoints! In Part E: -3 < x < 1. x is simultaneously greater than -3 AND less than 1! On the number line, shade the region strictly BETWEEN -3 and 1. Since both symbols are strict (<), use parentheses on both ends: (-3, 1)!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part F: 2 ≤ x < 3. Here, 2 is included (≤), but 3 is excluded (<)! At 2, place a solid bracket [ . At 3, place an open parenthesis ) . Shade between 2 and 3. In interval notation: [2, 3)! This is called a half-open interval. In agricultural quality control, grain moisture content must often fall into bounded intervals like this to prevent spoilage during winter storage.\nTA Sora: In agricultural applications across the Gallatin Valley, grain moisture content for winter wheat storage must be strictly between 12% and 14%: 12 < m < 14. If moisture exceeds 14%, grain molds and can spontaneously combust in grain elevators. If moisture drops below 12%, grain kernels crack during milling. Bounded intervals like (-3, 1) and [2, 3) define the safe operational zones in biology, chemistry, and manufacturing.",
    "5": "Prof. Park: On slide 5, we examine Section 1.9 Examples 1G and 1H: Disjoint Compound Inequalities using the Union Symbol (∪): Part G: x < -7 or x ≥ 0. Part H: x ≤ 2 or x > 5.\nTA Sora: When you see the word 'OR', the solution set consists of two separate, non-overlapping intervals! In Part G: x < -7 OR x ≥ 0. First piece: x < -7 is (-∞, -7). Second piece: x ≥ 0 is [0, ∞). To combine them into a single mathematical statement, we use the UNION operator ∪ (which looks like a capital U): (-∞, -7) ∪ [0, ∞)!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part H: x ≤ 2 or x > 5. Left piece: x ≤ 2 gives (-∞, 2]. Right piece: x > 5 gives (5, ∞). United together: (-∞, 2] ∪ (5, ∞). The union symbol represents 'taking everything in either set.'\nProf. Park: Notice the union symbol ∪ in compound inequalities: In computer programming and database querying (like SQL), ∪ represents the OR operator. If a point belongs to either the left interval OR the right interval, it is accepted into the solution set! In contrast, the intersection symbol ∩ (an upside-down U) represents AND—where intervals overlap.",
    "6": "Prof. Park: Slide 6 moves to Section 1.9 Examples 2A and 2B on workbook page 26: Solving Linear Inequalities algebraically: Part A: -6 + 3x ≤ -12 + 5x. Part B: 4(5x - 7) > 10 + 5x.\nTA Sora: Solving inequalities follows the exact same balance scale steps as solving equations, with one exception: watch out for dividing by a negative! Let's solve Part A: -6 + 3x ≤ -12 + 5x. Subtract 5x from both sides: -6 - 2x ≤ -12. Add 6 to both sides: -2x ≤ -6. Now divide both sides by -2: DANGER! We are dividing by NEGATIVE 2! FLIP the symbol from ≤ to ≥! x ≥ (-6) / (-2) = 3! So x ≥ 3. In interval notation: [3, ∞)!\nProf. Park: Now let's solve Part B: 4(5x - 7) > 10 + 5x. Distribute the 4: 20x - 28 > 10 + 5x. Subtract 5x from both sides: 15x - 28 > 10. Add 28 to both sides: 15x > 38. Divide by positive 15: since 15 is positive, do NOT flip the symbol! x > 38/15. In interval notation: (38/15, ∞)!\nTA Sora: In Example 2A: -6 + 3x ≤ -12 + 5x, what if a student subtracted 3x instead of 5x? Let's see: subtract 3x: -6 ≤ -12 + 2x. Add 12: 6 ≤ 2x. Divide by positive 2: 3 ≤ x! Reading from x: x ≥ 3! Notice that by moving the variable to the right, the coefficient was positive (+2), so they never had to divide by a negative or flip the sign! Both algebraic paths lead to the exact same interval [3, ∞)!",
    "7": "Prof. Park: Slide 7 presents Section 1.9 Examples 2C and 2D on workbook page 26: Inequalities with Fractions and Negative Denominators: Part C: (4/5)y + 5 < 6. Part D: 2 ≤ x / (-8) - 3.\nTA Sora: In Part C: (4/5)y + 5 < 6. Subtract 5 from both sides: (4/5)y < 1. Multiply both sides by reciprocal 5/4: y < 5/4. In interval notation: (-∞, 5/4)!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part D: 2 ≤ x / (-8) - 3. Add 3 to both sides: 5 ≤ x / (-8). To clear the denominator, multiply both sides by -8! DANGER! Multiplying by -8 FLIPS the inequality symbol from ≤ to ≥! 5 · (-8) ≥ x, which gives: -40 ≥ x! Reading from x: x ≤ -40! In interval notation: (-∞, -40]! That symbol flip was critical!\nProf. Park: In Example 2D: 2 ≤ x / (-8) - 3, notice how clearing the negative denominator flips the symbol: 5 ≤ x / (-8) multiplied by -8 gives -40 ≥ x, which is x ≤ -40! Let's check with a number in the interval, say x = -48: Right side: -48 / -8 - 3 = 6 - 3 = 3. Is 2 ≤ 3? Yes, 2 is less than 3! The inequality holds true!",
    "8": "Prof. Park: Slide 8 features Section 1.9 Examples 2E and 2F on workbook page 26: A side-by-side comparison of positive vs. negative denominators: Part E: (x - 30) / (-4) ≥ 50. Part F: (x - 30) / 4 > 50.\nTA Sora: Look at Part E: (x - 30) / (-4) ≥ 50. Multiply both sides by -4: this FLIPS the symbol from ≥ to ≤! x - 30 ≤ 50 · (-4) = -200. Add 30 to both sides: x ≤ -200 + 30 = -170! Interval notation: (-∞, -170]!\nProf. Park: Now look at Part F: (x - 30) / 4 > 50. Multiply both sides by +4: since 4 is positive, do NOT flip the symbol! x - 30 > 50 · 4 = 200. Add 30 to both sides: x > 230! Interval notation: (230, ∞)! Look at the dramatic difference: Part E yields numbers below -170, while Part F yields numbers above +230! That single negative sign completely inverted the entire solution domain!\nTA Sora: Look at the visual contrast between Examples 2E and 2F: In 2E, dividing by -4 gives x ≤ -170: (-∞, -170]. In 2F, dividing by +4 gives x > 230: (230, ∞). That comparison shows why attention to negative signs is the hallmark of a true mathematician.",
    "9": "Prof. Park: Slide 9 concludes Lecture 15 and marks the completion of Unit 1! Let's celebrate everyone's incredible dedication across Sections 1.0 through 1.9 with Sora's 10 Core Commandments of Algebra!\nTA Sora: Here are our 10 Golden Commandments: 1. Order of Operations: PEMDAS always rules—parentheses, exponents, multiply/divide left to right, add/subtract left to right! 2. Substitution: Always wrap substituted numbers in protective parentheses to preserve signs! 3. Exponents: Product rule adds powers, quotient rule subtracts powers, negative exponents take the elevator! 4. Distributive Property: Watch minus signs in front of parentheses—they flip every sign inside! 5. Polynomial Multiplication: Binomial squared has a middle term! (a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2! 6. Rational Expressions: Add and subtract ONLY over a common denominator! 7. Linear Equations: The balance scale rule—isolate the variable using inverse operations! 8. Clearing Fractions: Multiply every term on both sides by the LCD to eliminate all fractions in one step! 9. Equation Classification: Conditional (one solution), Identity (all real numbers), Contradiction (no solution)! 10. Inequalities: FLIP the inequality symbol whenever you multiply or divide by a negative number!\nProf. Park: With these 10 commandments etched into your algebraic toolbox, you are 100% prepared for Unit 2: Linear Equations in Two Variables and Graphing. Congratulations on conquering Unit 1 of M090 Introductory Algebra!\nProf. Park: As we close Unit 1, let's look at the incredible ground we have covered: From fractions, real numbers, and exponents in Sections 1.0 to 1.2, to polynomials and FOIL in Sections 1.3 and 1.4, to rational expressions in Section 1.5, and finally linear equations, clearing fractions, literal formulas, and inequalities in Sections 1.6 through 1.9! You have built an unshakeable mathematical foundation. In Unit 2, we will bring all of this algebra to life on the two-dimensional Cartesian plane. Thank you for your fantastic dedication, and we will see you in Unit 2!"
}
